@page "/docs/primitives"

<DocsPage Title="Introduction" Description="Unstyled Blazor components for design systems and web apps.">
    <p>
        <code>Blazix.Primitives</code> is the layer the styled components are built on. Each primitive handles behaviour and
        accessibility (ARIA, keyboard navigation, focus management, dismissal) and renders no styles of its own. It has Base
        UI's parts, data attributes and CSS variables, so you can style it with Tailwind, plain CSS, CSS isolation or
        anything else.
    </p>

    <DocsHeading Title="Assemble a component" />
    <p>
        This demo assembles a <a href="/docs/primitives/popover">Popover</a>'s parts and styles them with Tailwind. The
        primitives are unstyled, so plain CSS, CSS isolation or any other way of styling works just as well.
    </p>
    <ComponentPreview Name="primitives/popover-hero" />

    <DocsHeading Title="Next steps" />
    <p>
        <a href="/docs/primitives/installation">Install the library</a> and set up your app. The handbook then explains how to
        <a href="/docs/primitives/styling">style</a>, <a href="/docs/primitives/animation">animate</a>,
        <a href="/docs/primitives/composition">compose</a> and <a href="/docs/primitives/customization">customize</a> the
        primitives, and what they do for <a href="/docs/primitives/accessibility">accessibility</a>. Each primitive's page
        lists its parts, with their parameters, data attributes and CSS variables.
    </p>
    <p>
        Want them styled? The <a href="/docs">components</a> are built on these primitives, and the CLI copies them into
        your project as source you own.
    </p>

    <DocsHeading Title="All Primitives" />
    <ComponentsList Pages="DocsNav.Primitives" />
</DocsPage>
